WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.720
Hey! Welcome to a new Project Kamp update!

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.199
In this video, we're going to show you some sensors, 
that we've installed on the land.

00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:11.640
Including this wind meter.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.720
And, also we're going to talk more about all the electronics,
and how we hooked everything up together.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.225
We're going to show you a map, because we're currently mapping out 
all the native trees on the land!

00:00:21.225 --> 00:00:23.360
To think about the future of our landscape.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.480
So, get ready for some geeky stuff.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:41.640
Hello, guys!

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:42.730
- Hello!

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:43.328
Who are you?

00:00:43.328 --> 00:00:44.728
- I'm Naomi!

00:00:45.014 --> 00:00:46.401
I'm Ceren!

00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:49.960
I'm going to be here for two months, 
and I'm from the Netherlands.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
I'm going to be logging native trees in the area!

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.080
We're now up the rock, with Basekamp down there.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:03.960
I think it would be great to show all the native trees in the area, 
in contrast to all the mimosa and eucalyptus.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.720
And, in the future, they might want to build stuff.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:12.726
I recommend not to build next to native trees, 
because you might need to chop them...

00:01:12.726 --> 00:01:14.880
You want to regenerate the whole land.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.560
I just picked a waypoint app,
so I'm going to be standing next to a tree...

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.920
Wait until the GPS locates me...

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.480
And then, I create a waypoint, add some information...

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.160
Like, the name, the height of the tree, and give it a color code.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.520
And then, we'll walk to the next one.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.560
- We have Ziggy here for support, right?

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.976
Mental support!

00:01:36.314 --> 00:01:37.720
- Just eating the trees.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.200
Yeah, we tried to teach him to eat 
eucalyptus and the mimosa.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.680
You just like sticks a lot?

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.600
- You think that will solve the problem
of Project Kamp with the mimosas?

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.000
We might need a lot of dogs...

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.179
- Just dogs!

00:01:49.556 --> 00:01:50.544
All right, let's do it!

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:51.760
- Cool!

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.960
This is the app, and the blue spot shows where we are.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.692
This green area is Basekamp.

00:02:00.211 --> 00:02:02.352
Here, we have the old ruins.

00:02:03.157 --> 00:02:05.746
We just walked up here.

00:02:05.746 --> 00:02:07.936
I'll show you the logs I did now...

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.280
The walk to Basekamp is all mimosa forest.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.796
And then, the first native tree I saw I spotted.

00:02:13.796 --> 00:02:16.088
And just, like, logged everything I saw!

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.935
The different color codes, like the red ones,
are the strawberry trees.

00:02:20.935 --> 00:02:22.680
Green ones are the oak trees.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.240
English oak.

00:02:25.357 --> 00:02:28.880
Pink is a cork tree, which we really love here.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.600
And, the pine trees are the black ones.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.461
It's fun to see that the whole line
is already like the border of our land. 

00:02:36.661 --> 00:02:38.891
But, this one, it's all like planted eucalyptus trees.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.600
So, I just worked my way up till the big rock.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.740
And this was my first area,

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.420
the app is called Tracklia.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:53.600
Next to the Tracklia app, for the waypoints, I use Pl@ntnet,
and it is an identifying app.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.520
You can download it for free, and put your location in.

00:02:58.507 --> 00:03:00.520
And, so, we're in Europe now.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.800
You can add photos from your gallery,
or even like take them new.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.689
You add a picture like from a leaf!

00:03:07.689 --> 00:03:13.640
You can add like it's a leaf or fruit, bark, or flower,
and it helps identify the specific species.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.549
- What are strawberry trees?

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:19.520
Strawberry trees are bush-like trees.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.960
We can back up in a bit.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:27.520
It has different branches, and the little fruits
are like spiky strawberry like fruits.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.600
And, I think the name is just like
because the fruit turns red.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:31.902
It looks like a strawberry,

00:03:31.902 --> 00:03:33.021
- But, you can't eat them, right?

00:03:33.021 --> 00:03:33.967
I'm actually not sure.

00:03:33.967 --> 00:03:34.673
- You can!

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:36.143
You can?

00:03:36.143 --> 00:03:36.964
That's fun!

00:03:36.964 --> 00:03:38.312
- They stick to your teeth.

00:03:38.312 --> 00:03:40.721
Oh, we should try them, if they're better than strawberries!

00:03:41.511 --> 00:03:42.722
- Are they tasty?

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.311
I haven't tried, and we'll definitely try now!

00:04:01.206 --> 00:04:02.859
These are little fruits!

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.535
- I think I've seen them already!

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:06.450
They're turning already!

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.360
- They're turning red already.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.909
They have to be soft.

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:12.152
- A strawberry tree!

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.960
And, that's amazing for a woodcarving,
if you want to make a spoon or something.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:23.800
- Did you bite on it?

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.034
Yeah.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.520
It doesn't even look like a strawberry.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.322
- It doesn't, does it?

00:04:32.716 --> 00:04:33.720
Sorry, if that's on camera.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.065
Please don't put it in...

00:04:36.856 --> 00:04:38.661
Ziggy, what tree is that?

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:40.919
Is that an oak?

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:42.568
Mimosa.

00:04:42.568 --> 00:04:44.000
We try to teach him not to eat the oak...

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.240
Only mimosa.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.200
What do we have there?

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.042
It's an oak?

00:05:00.042 --> 00:05:01.339
- It’s an English oak.

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:08.720
And, you can see the light green, short stalks,
at the beginning of the stalk.

00:05:09.527 --> 00:05:13.320
The most common one in the land here.

00:05:17.455 --> 00:05:19.015
This is a cork tree.

00:05:19.015 --> 00:05:24.720
This is the burned mother tree, 
and it has new growth.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.760
So, we will log this.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.600
I will stand next to it as close as I can...

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.320
Get the GPS going again...

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.480
Make a waypoint...

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Cork tree.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.511
Oak, sorry.

00:05:45.498 --> 00:05:51.298
And then, it's logged right there!

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.680
- Are there many cork trees left here?

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.600
Yeah, actually, I would say quite a lot, like a few big ones.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.160
You can actually see the bark has been burnt.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.640
Or, it has been burnt, but around it all new ones sprout.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.360
I wouldn't say it is the most common tree here...

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.480
But, yeah, they could come back,
so I'm quite optimistic about these!

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.160
- All right! Let's go to the next one!

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:24.472
There is a pine.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:25.200
- There's pine!

00:06:26.083 --> 00:06:28.397
Quite a tall one, I would say.

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:35.239
And then, as close as I can, I will make a waypoint.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.560
I chose the color black for this icon and saved it.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:41.462
There you have it!

00:06:41.462 --> 00:06:43.037
- Bueno!

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:50.458
What is this?

00:06:50.458 --> 00:06:51.920
- This is a Pyrenees oak.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.880
So, you can differentiate between the English oak,
and the Pyrenees by looking at the leaves.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.400
This is quite velvety, almost like hairy, 
especially on the back.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.480
It feels very soft, and the color is a bit
more a grayish-green.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.560
And, that is one of the characteristics of the Pyrenees oak.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.520
We're here at the English oak.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.520
These are called plant galls.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.520
They're actually like abnormal growths in the oak.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:27.360
They're caused by various parasites,
such as fungi, or bacteria, mites, or even wasps.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.240
They're actually empty now.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:35.280
They start out really green,
and they dry out with a very papery feeling.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.200
They're quite common in oaks.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.040
So, yeah, this is the terracotta pot I just found.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:47.440
These can be found all up in the mimosa forest,
and, like, up the big rock.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.840
They're all broken, but they used to be used 
to have them for gathering all the resin from the mimosa.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.112
So, if you cut the mimosa bark,
it slowly, like, pours out resin.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:01.762
It's actually quite nice!

00:08:01.762 --> 00:08:03.500
We find them, and we take them back to the Kamp.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:06.863
We use them for like slow watering,
and put them next to plants and stuff.

00:08:06.863 --> 00:08:08.653
So, we try to gather as much as we can.

00:08:08.991 --> 00:08:10.387
- They are burnt, right?

00:08:10.387 --> 00:08:11.511
They’re burnt, yeah...

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:13.238
- From the last fire.

00:08:13.238 --> 00:08:15.790
Yeah, in 2018, if I remember?

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:16.556
- 2017.

00:08:16.556 --> 00:08:17.056
2017?

00:08:17.056 --> 00:08:17.816
- Yeah.

00:08:17.816 --> 00:08:18.478
So, most of them are burnt, when we find them.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.760
This is what you do?

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:36.511
- Yes! This is what I do all day!

00:08:37.186 --> 00:08:43.200
Yeah, so, knowing that the path we just walked
is the border of the land.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.600
Down there is the road.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:51.000
So, I just need to get like zigzag
through every meter of the mimosa forest.

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:55.560
Knowing that the mimosa represses
other species growth...

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.840
We won't find any other species,
than just maybe some eucalyptus.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Sometimes, some thorns, like brambles.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.745
But, just to be safe as a good ecologist, 
you need to get like every meter.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.320
So, I'll just continue zig-zagging my way through this area.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.000
- All right!

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:51.400
That's it for today, huh?

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:52.800
- That's it for today!

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.160
We did a square today.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.560
A square’s a hectare.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.960
It wasn't really a dense area,
so we could get a lot done today.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:07.480
Yeah, I would say that the whole project area 
is around more than ten hectares.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.849
I hope to get like all the logging
 of the native trees done...

00:10:10.849 --> 00:10:14.560
But, it will be a very hard project to do.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.560
Some hectares are very packed in native trees.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.912
The one we just did this afternoon,
 or this morning wasn't really dense...

00:10:21.912 --> 00:10:24.538
It was mostly mimosa forest.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.280
So, yeah, it's going to be a lot of work, but a lot of fun.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.760
Hey! I'm Jerry!

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:33.800
I'm from the Netherlands!

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.400
I've been here for a week,
to install a whole monitoring system.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:44.823
So, the last season, Dave installed the solar panels, 
and all of these electronics.

00:10:44.823 --> 00:10:50.680
And, back then, he already mentioned that it's important
 to be able to get some statistics out of this.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:58.253
Because, here at Project Kamp,
we want to measure different aspects of off grid living

00:10:58.253 --> 00:11:02.360
We want to see how the land grows, and how it evolves.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:10.880
So, to make a uniform platform for this, 
I installed a Raspberry Pi running Home Assistant.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:18.360
And, that is really a big open source project, 
which is made for home automation.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.960
So, to capture a lot of data sensor input.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:25.280
And based on that, also control different aspects.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:29.120
So, with that, we know how much energy we use...

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.160
How much solar power we are generating...

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.200
And, how much is still left in the battery, for example.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.840
Over time, we can add more sensors,
like a weather station, which we will show in a bit.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:48.920
But, also, more sensors like a water well sensor,
to see if we still have enough water.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:55.960
And, to keep all of this organized,
we made a display inside of the kitchen container.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:05.410
Where we show if the battery is charging, how much AC power 
we are using, and how much is still left in the battery.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:15.759
So, for that, we mounted this display,
where you can see that currently the battery is at 63%.

00:12:15.759 --> 00:12:20.814
And, it's in the green this arrow, 
so that means that the battery is now charging.

00:12:22.217 --> 00:12:25.640
That's because the solar is generating a lot of energy.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.640
So, in the morning it was kind of cloudy,
but now the weather is getting better.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:36.722
We're generating more electricity, 
and we're basically not consuming any AC power,

00:12:36.722 --> 00:12:37.720
Not a lot!

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:43.660
So, it's mainly just the fridge being on,
and some tools being used on the ruin.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.502
The coolest thing here is that you see that there 
is zero kilowatt hours from the grid!

00:12:48.502 --> 00:12:51.120
Because, we are basically just fully off grid!

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:55.360
So, we really rely on solar energy,
and energy stored in the battery.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:04.560
And, with this system, we can basically track
a lot of different sensors and different setups.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:12.600
So, we also implemented a weather station,
where we can see how the ground temperature is evolving...

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:18.880
How much light is being shined 
on different parts of the land...

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.760
And also, the soil.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:24.760
So, if the soil is very dry or if it's very wet.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:32.640
Lately, it's been very dry, because basically the last two weeks
 have been super hot and with no rain at all.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:37.320
And, over time, we also want to track
different, more manual settings.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.720
Manual sensors, as you wish.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:46.400
So, for example, the amount of people
at one time at Project Kamp...

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.760
The times that we've seen foxes...

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.920
The amount of gas bottles that we still use...

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.840
Because, this is the only fossil fuel,
that we are still relying on.

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:59.120
And also, the amount of water that we consume.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.920
That goes through the system,
and goes back into the well.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.840
Here, we have a small sensor setup.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:13.320
So, it's being powered by a small solar panel,
and this generates all the electricity that the sensor needs.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:19.480
And then, once every 15 minutes, 
it sends it to the Raspberry Pi, that I showed in the back.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:22.520
And, that really captures and logs all the data.

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:26.240
This is a small food container box.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.440
We thought it would be useful to use, to keep it waterproof.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.360
And, these are just very simple microcontrollers.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:42.600
So, we really want to create a platform that different people
 can create different sensors to measure different aspects of the land.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:47.520
Okay, so, that's everything
about the weather station here.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:50.920
Over time, there will be more sensors, more statistics...

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.360
But, this is a nice first introduction.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:15:00.000
And, basically, from here on,
we will keep you up to date in the future.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:04.320
For now, enjoy the rest of the video and see you.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:11.960
So, one sensor we've installed, before Jerry came,
 with all these geeky sensors is this wind meter.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.560
It measures the strength of the wind.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:21.080
The main reason for installing this was to check 
if it's worth it to add a wind turbine to generate electricity.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:26.080
So, we actually already installed it last year 
on the top of the highest point of the land.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:31.720
But, we realized whatever electricity we generate there, 
we're probably not going to put a wind turbine there.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:34.560
Because, we need to pull the cable
all the way to Basekamp.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:36.970
So, since a few months, we put it here in Basekamp.

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:41.720
Because, it would be way more realistic,
 if we get electricity to actually hook it up to our system.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.480
And, it probably would be more of a small wind turbine...

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:49.200
There was more wind up the rock,
but, yeah, it's harder to get the electricity....

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.280
So, here, it would be a smaller turbine,
with probably bigger blades, but a small motor.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.560
So, here you can see a simulation of what
a three meter blade would look like.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.877
And overall, this project is some research done by From Waste to Wind.

00:16:01.877 --> 00:16:08.480
They're actually with Precious Plastic trying to make a wind turbine,
from recycled plastic and using 3D printing.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.920
You can find more about that research here.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:14.448
But yeah, overall, it's an ongoing topic.

00:16:14.448 --> 00:16:19.080
And ideally, also, we hooked this one up to our system,
that we have it digitally and synched.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.440
So, it's easier to extract all the data.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.440
This is still generation one.

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:47.581
Hello!

00:16:47.581 --> 00:16:49.680
Today, we are in São Joaninho.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.840
Daniel invited us on his property to pick oranges.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:58.000
Because, he's working in the United Nations,
and he's not here to pick them.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.600
They just rot away.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:05.640
So, he sent us an email and invited us,
and we're going to pick some fresh oranges.

00:17:11.814 --> 00:17:13.294
We have Charan!

00:17:14.514 --> 00:17:15.956
We have Julie!

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:19.440
And, we have Ted! 

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:21.760
And, me, of course, Felix.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.400
So, you have it a lot in Portugal that people
 have orange trees on their property.

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:48.760
And, since the young people move away, or I don't know, 
go for work in the big cities and just not get picked.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:55.880
So, in a lot of places, you have a lot of orange trees,
with a lot of falling oranges.

00:17:56.680 --> 00:18:00.000
Fresh and nice to eat, but no one picks them.

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:51.200
Here, we have all the picked oranges.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:54.200
Took us maybe 10 minutes,

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:55.160
Quite a lot!

00:18:55.160 --> 00:19:01.120
There are still some left in the tree, 
but I guess that's kind of everything we need for the moment!

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:04.960
So, thank you, Daniel, for the oranges!

00:19:15.394 --> 00:19:19.040
Of course, we use oranges after plucking them.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:24.600
We made some, some orange juice for breakfast,
and we made an orange cake.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:30.320
You can make many more things with oranges, 
and I think we're also going to look into preserving the oranges.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:33.162
So, for instance, drying them by making a drying rack.

00:19:33.162 --> 00:19:37.120
For drying the peels, and drying maybe all of the orange,
in order to preserve them.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:44.600
To save as much oranges as we can from the trees, 
that are producing them, but the consumers that are not there anymore.

